1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,849
Paris, twelve ninety-two.

2
00:00:01,799 --> 00:00:02,841
It is Saturday,

3
00:00:02,791 --> 00:00:05,317
and you are going to the baths.

4
00:00:05,427 --> 00:00:07,436
Not the river. A bathhouse.

5
00:00:07,386 --> 00:00:10,262
This city has at least two dozen.

6
00:00:10,373 --> 00:00:13,083
But nobody may cry that the water is hot

7
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:15,659
until it is day. That is the law.

8
00:00:15,609 --> 00:00:17,220
Whoever gets up in the dark

9
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:18,429
to answer that cry

10
00:00:18,379 --> 00:00:20,110
walks into a dark street,

11
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:22,394
and the street is the danger.

12
00:00:22,468 --> 00:00:24,650
So you wait for first light.

13
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:26,778
Then the crier comes down your street:

14
00:00:26,728 --> 00:00:28,590
the baths are hot.

15
00:00:28,664 --> 00:00:30,733
Two deniers buys the steam room:

16
00:00:30,683 --> 00:00:33,586
wooden benches, a furnace behind the wall,

17
00:00:33,536 --> 00:00:34,929
air you can see.

18
00:00:34,879 --> 00:00:37,345
Four deniers buys a tub.

19
00:00:37,444 --> 00:00:38,727
Tomorrow is Sunday.

20
00:00:38,677 --> 00:00:40,952
On Sunday, no bathhouse in Paris

21
00:00:40,902 --> 00:00:42,835
may light its fire.

22
00:00:42,909 --> 00:00:45,149
So today you sit in the steam,

23
00:00:45,099 --> 00:00:47,096
beside people from the market,

24
00:00:47,046 --> 00:00:50,516
and a week of smoke comes off your skin.

25
00:00:50,597 --> 00:00:52,365
Medieval people washed.

26
00:00:52,315 --> 00:00:55,034
They just had to wait for daylight.
